    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d400">

                <pb facs="0551" n="537" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg537"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.24/5–3145: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Braden</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-31T20:00:00-03:00">May 31, 1945—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1111. FEA states Dept. has requested FEA drop slowdown policy and expedite export
                    licenses for Argentina. Request slowdown policy be continued for time being in
                    view considerations contained my 1093, May 30, 5 p.m.<anchor n="64"
                        xml:id="d400-f1"/> regarding desirability continuation Proclaimed and
                    Confidential Lists for Argentina.</p>
                <p>In other words I feel we should only supply Argentina in degree to which she
                    genuinely implements her Chapultepec obligations.<anchor n="65" xml:id="d400-f2"
                    /> To this policy I would make following exceptions of materials which [we]
                    should supply so liberally as our war needs allow: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>real requirements for health and well being of Argentine
                            people;</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>materials which others will furnish if we do not, i.e., don’t bite off
                            our noses to spite our face;</item>
                        <pb facs="0552" n="538" xml:id="pg538"/>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>exports covered by points of Dept.’s tel. 554, May 19, 2 p.m.,
                            although careful scrutiny should be given to those coming under point
                            3.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Braden</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d400-f1" n="64" xml:id="d400-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d400-f2" n="65" xml:id="d400-f2-content">
                <p>For texts of Resolutions agreed upon by delegates to the Chapultepec Conference,
                    see Pan American Union, <hi rendition="#italic">Final Act of the Inter-American
                        Conference</hi> on <hi rendition="#italic">Problems of War and Peace, Mexico
                        City, February–March 1945</hi> (Washington, 1945), pp. 35–108. For
                    documentation on this Conference, see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg1">pp. 1</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
